26: \begin{document}
27: \maketitle
28: 
29: \begin{abstract}
30: Formal mathematics projects increasingly use AI agents for drafting,
31: review, theorem search, and manuscript preparation.  Ungated agent output,
32: however, can create theorem-looking but content-poor edits, stale source
33: references, duplicated submission routes, and premature paper promotion.  We
34: report an auditable theory-to-paper pipeline used around a Lean-backed
35: BEDC source workspace and an automath publication workspace.  The
36: pipeline separates source development, intake packets, active paper
37: directories, AI review loops, deterministic gates, and human promotion
38: decisions.  Its central rule is that AI output is advisory: load-bearing
39: mathematical and submission claims must be tied, at the appropriate evidence
40: level, to source paths, command logs, deterministic checks, and human-approved
41: promotion.  Under record-gate soundness, the core interface theorem constructs
42: the finite publication certificates checked by the package verifier from recorded
43: gate-passage entries and explicitly submitted review surfaces; its safety claim is
44: deliberately certificate-level and finite-record only.  The theorem is not an
45: implementation-soundness theorem, a formal-verification theorem, an
46: artifact-semantic theorem, or a venue-acceptance theorem: it proves neither Lean
47: builds, daemon behaviour, artifact semantics, nor venue acceptance.  The current
48: package then proves schema-relative, row-frozen finite-record certificate exactness
49: for its six-row, path-verified instantiation only, while rejecting stronger readings
50: such as external gate implementation soundness, proof-assistant correctness,
51: artifact semantic validity, current execution of every gate, or venue acceptance.
52: Four case
53: studies illustrate the gates at the path-verified historical-snapshot level: intake
54: isolation, overlap/submitted blocking, fake-extension rejection, and
55: artifact-limitation disclosure.
56: The scoped boundary normal form isolates the five presentation-package weak
57: points---record-gate soundness, imported source-interface evidence, finite
58: witness rows, primary-claim inventories, and theorem inventories---and proves that
59: each supports only its bounded finite-record reading unless a fresh finite upgrade
60: coordinate is supplied.  In particular, any end-to-end workflow-correctness
61: reading decomposes into separate finite budgets for gate implementation audits,
62: fresh formal/source checks, artifact semantics, and submitted-support records; the
63: present package intentionally supplies the finite-record certificate level rather
64: than those stronger budgets.
65: \end{abstract}
66: 
67: \section{Problem and contribution}
68: 
69: AI assistance is useful in a formal-mathematics project only if the project can
70: distinguish proposal from evidence.  A generated theorem statement, a legal
71: piece of code, a mechanical expansion, or a lower score in a review rubric does
72: not by itself constitute mathematical discovery.  The practical publication
73: failure modes are equally structural: a route may overlap a submitted sibling,
74: an artifact status file may drift from generated counts, or an intake note may
75: accidentally become a daemon-visible manuscript.
76: 
77: This source is the technical supplement for the presentation-only contribution
78: \emph{An Auditable Theory Compiler for AI-Assisted Formal Mathematics}, whose
79: single primary artifact is the compact source \path{submission_abstract.tex}.
80: This file is subordinate technical support, not a competing submission title.
81: The final source-level packaging convention used throughout both sources is the
82: following: the primary CICM presentation-only article is
83: \path{submission_abstract.tex}; this file, \path{main.tex}, is the local
84: technical supplement; \path{theorem_inventory.json} is the local bounded
85: theorem-obligation inventory; and \path{review_bundle/} is the local support
86: directory.  No venue-upload receipt, DOI-bearing replacement, or final
87: archive-equivalent byte-equality locator is claimed in this source package.
88: The short artifact states the workshop-facing thesis and boundary; this
89: supplement gives finite-record definitions, theorem proofs, and case-evidence
90: tables; and the support directory supplies local/path-verified records only until
91: a separate finite upload or archive-equivalence coordinate is added.  This file
92: records a compact workflow pattern for avoiding these failures: an auditable
93: theory-to-paper pipeline in which AI agents propose, review, and triage, while
94: deterministic gates decide what may be treated as load-bearing evidence.  The
95: pattern is instantiated in local BEDC and automath workspaces.
96: \paragraph{Reader map.}
97: The supplement has two layers.  The \emph{core architecture/interface chain}
98: contains Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} (finite
99: certificate tuple and dispatch),
100: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} and
101: Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}
102: (record-gate input/output and non-claim boundary),
103: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} (six finite acceptance
104: predicates), Theorems~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract},
105: \ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, and
106: \ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} (schema-projection exactness
107: and non-escalation), Theorem~\ref{thm:auditable-compiler-boundary-completeness}
108: (input/output channels of the compiler contribution),
109: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} (typed record-interface rigidity),
110: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} (finite publication-certificate
111: invariants under record-gate soundness),
112: Theorem~\ref{thm:external-interface-projection-no-free-upgrade} (no free
113: implementation-soundness upgrade), and
114: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-rigidity} (bad-subrecord normal form).
115: These are the theorem chain supporting the CICM software-architecture claim.
116: 
117: The second layer is \emph{support-boundary instantiation}: package shape,
118: file-existence, digest, upload/archive, command-run, venue, theorem-inventory,
119: and current-package fixed-point statements.  Those labelled results are finite
120: record lemmas or audit invariants for this source package.  They keep the
121: submission boundary explicit, but they are subordinate to the architecture chain
122: and are not independent claims about gate implementations, artifact semantics,
123: Lean/BEDC rebuilds, or venue state.  For the presentation version these labels
124: compress to the finite support tuple of
125: Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-invariant-compression}; full label coverage is
126: carried by \path{theorem_inventory.json}, the theorem-environment extractor log,
127: the finite record-verifier logs, and the final digest table rather than by
128: repeating every support-projection theorem or route-normal-form classifier in the
129: compact primary artifact.  The route-level support mode is primary-only external
130: submission with local/path-verified support: this supplement and
131: \path{review_bundle/} are not asserted to be uploaded support unless a later
132: finite upload receipt or archive-equivalence byte-equality coordinate is added.
133: The result is intentionally not a proof of external implementation-level
134: soundness: Lean builds, publication-daemon behaviour, artifact semantics, and
135: fresh command runs enter the argument only when a finite certificate records the
136: corresponding evidence surface and boundary.  Root-level diagnostic files outside
137: the primary article, this supplement, and the local support bundle are treated
138: below only as local historical or path-verified context unless a displayed
139: certificate explicitly places the corresponding record on the support surface.
140: 
141: \paragraph{Notation and scope boundary.}
142: Here \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\) abbreviates record-gate soundness on the
143: finite submitted surface \(S\), package \(P\), ledger \(L\), and dispatch table
144: \(\Gamma\).  The stronger-reading preorder \(c\preceq_{\Gamma}c^{+}\) means that
145: \(c^{+}\) asserts every finite coordinate of \(c\) and at least one additional
146: axis, evidence level, semantic, implementation, venue, or support coordinate
147: assigned by \(\Gamma\).  A finite bad subrecord is an inclusion-minimal subset of
148: the submitted finite fields that witnesses the first failed acceptance predicate.
149: A support-coordinate fixed point is a support tuple whose inventory bytes,
150: verifier inputs, verifier logs, and digest rows name one another at the same
151: source state.  A no-free-upgrade result says that a stronger reading is accepted
152: only after its positive finite coordinate is added and passed.
153: 
154: The supplement is deliberately limited to auditable theory-to-paper workflow
155: claims for formal mathematics.  It does not propose a theorem prover, a Lean
156: hammer, a complete BEDC theory, an automated journal-acceptance method, or a
157: claim that AI output is proof evidence.  Some logistical facts are stated in
158: proposition or theorem environments because they are finite-record consequences
159: used by the certificate calculus; those statements should be read as conditional
160: audit-record claims over printed paths, tables, manifests, and logs.  The
161: mathematical obligations are discharged by the displayed TeX definitions,
162: theorems, and proofs in the core chain, not by the theorem inventory, upload
163: bookkeeping, digest records, or venue-boundary records.
164: 
165: \begin{proposition}[Final package-shape convention]
166: \label{prop:final-package-shape-convention}
167: For the current presentation package there is a single source-level package
168: shape.  The primary article is \path{submission_abstract.tex}; the local
169: technical supplement is \path{main.tex}; the local bounded theorem-obligation
170: ledger consists of \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md};
171: and the local support surface is the finite tuple \path{theorem_inventory.json},
172: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, and \path{review_bundle/} with the final digest
173: record \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  The package records no
174: DOI-bearing replacement, venue-upload receipt, final archive-equivalent
175: byte-equality locator, or alternate external support directory.  Consequently
176: every bundle-backed support claim in this source is a local/path-verified support
177: claim unless a later finite upload receipt or archive-equivalence byte-equality
178: record is added.
179: \end{proposition}
180: 
181: \begin{proof}
182: The assertion is a finite record comparison.  The primary source names
183: \path{submission_abstract.tex} as the primary presentation article and states
184: that \path{main.tex}, \path{theorem_inventory.json},
185: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, and \path{review_bundle/} are local/path-verified
186: support surfaces for the present route.  The opening package paragraph of this
187: supplement names the same coordinates and explicitly records that no venue-upload
188: receipt, DOI-bearing replacement, final archive-equivalent byte-equality locator,
189: or alternate external support directory is present.  The digest manifest
190: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} records the local finite byte table,
191: while the bundle manifest and support-boundary text record only candidate public
192: coordinates until byte equality is separately witnessed.  These finite source
193: records therefore agree on one package shape and one route-level support mode:
194: primary-only external submission with local/path-verified support.
195: 
196: It remains only to identify the boundary of the convention.  A source-level
197: package-shape record fixes which files the manuscript treats as primary,
198: supplemental, and support material; it does not by itself prove that a venue
199: upload has occurred or that an archive-equivalent locator has been instantiated.
200: Hence bundle-backed claims remain local/path-verified unless a later finite
201: coordinate supplies an upload receipt or locator-plus-byte-equality record for the
202: same digest-identified tuple.  Since the current finite records contain no DOI,
203: venue-upload receipt, or public byte-equality receipt, none of those stronger
204: records can be inferred from the source.  This proves both the single package
205: convention and its local-support boundary.
206: \end{proof}
207: 
208: \begin{theorem}[Nested six-row and seven-row package states]
209: \label{thm:nested-package-states}
210: Let $P^{+}_{6}$ denote the current row-frozen certificate state whose accepted
211: rows are exactly $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ in
212: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}.  Let $P^{+}_{7}$ denote the enlarged
213: submitted support state obtained by adjoining the single theorem-ledger
214: row $a_7$ whose submitted coordinate is \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  Then
215: $P^{+}_{6}\subset P^{+}_{7}$ as finite record surfaces, and the inclusion is
216: conservative for the first six certificates: every acceptance decision, evidence
217: level, assigned-gate family, discovery-interface field, and command-boundary
218: field of rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ is the same in $P^{+}_{6}$ and $P^{+}_{7}$.  The
219: only new accepted claim of $P^{+}_{7}$ is the bounded theorem-obligation
220: ledger claim with syntactic label coverage for $a_7$.  In particular the two states are nested package states,